{"records":[{"id":"kc795mp93n","title":"Expression of negative co-stimulatory molecule B7-H3 in cervical cancer and precancerous lesions and its regulation on autophagy of cervical cancer cells","description":"Objective: To learn more about the expression of B7-H3 in cervical cancer and precancerous lesions and its regulatory effect on autophagy in cervical cancer cells. Methods: A total of 45 patients with cervical cancer who were treated in our hospital from July 2019 to June 2022 were collected as the cancer group. 229 patients with cervical intraepithelial neoplasia treated in our hospital during the same period were collected as the precancerous lesion group. Immunohistochemistry was used to detect the expression of B7-H3 in patients with cervical cancer. B7-H3 overexpression (LvB7-H3) and B7-H3 inhibitor (si-B7-H3) were constructed, and Western Blot analysis was used to assess the protein expression of B7-H3, ULK1, ATG13, FIP200, and LC3BII/I. Rapamycin and LvB7-H3 were co-intervened in cervical cancer cells to observe cell autophagy. Results: B7-H3 was mainly expressed in the cell membrane and cytoplasm of cancer cells, mainly in the cell membrane, and a small amount in the nucleus. Positive rates were significantly greater in the cancer group than in the precancerous lesion group (P=0.009). The 5-year survival rate did not differ significantly between positive and negative individuals (P=0.279). After transfection with LvB7-H3, the expression of p-mTOR and Ser757p-ULK1 was increased compared to Lv-NC, however the expression of p-mTOR and Ser757p-ULK1 was decreased when rapamycin was added to LvB7-H3 cells. Conclusions: B7-H3 contributes in cervical cancer autophagy through modulating the ULK1-ATG13-FIP200 complex via the mTOR signaling pathway, which may serve as a therapeutic target.\nKey words: B7-H3, cervical cancer, precancerous lesions, cervical cancer, autophagy\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-15T17:13:24.938Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kc795mp93n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kc795mp93n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sumei","last_name":"Niu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cervical Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zxpzhv2nkk","title":"ULK1/2 Regulates Stress Granule Disassembly Through Phosphorylation and Activation of VCP/p97","description":"Disturbances in autophagy and stress granule dynamics have been implicated as potential mechanisms underlying inclusion body myopathy (IBM) and related disorders. Yet, the roles of core autophagy proteins in IBM and stress granule dynamics remain poorly characterized. Here, we demonstrate that disrupted expression of the core autophagy proteins ULK1/2 in mice causes a vacuolar myopathy with ubiquitin and TDP-43-positive inclusions, similar to that caused by mutations in VCP, the most common cause of familial IBM. Mechanistically, we show that ULK1/2 localize to stress granules and phosphorylate VCP, thereby increasing VCP’s activity and ability to disassemble stress granules. These data suggest that dysregulation of VCP and stress granule disassembly contribute to IBM-like disease in Ulk1/2-deficient mice. In addition, stress granule disassembly is accelerated by an ULK1/2 agonist, suggesting ULK1/2 as targets for leveraging higher-order regulation of stress granules for therapeutic intervention in IBM and related disorders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-02-07T19:49:59.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zxpzhv2nkk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zxpzhv2nkk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mondira","last_name":"Kundu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bo","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dyy8xfvpkf","title":"Metamorphic proteins at the basis of human autophagy initiation and lipid transfer. Nguyen et al.","description":"Autophagy is a conserved intracellular degradation pathway that generates de novo double-membrane autophagosomes to target a wide range of material for lysosomal degradation. In multicellular organisms, autophagy initiation requires the timely assembly of a contact site between the ER and the nascent autophagosome. Here, we report the in vitro reconstitution of a full-length seven-subunit human autophagy initiation super-complex, build on a core complex of ATG13-101 and ATG9. Assembly of this core complex requires the rare ability of ATG13 and ATG101 to switch between distinct folds. The slow spontaneous metamorphic conversion is rate-limiting for the self-assembly of the super-complex. The interaction of the core complex with ATG2-WIPI4 enhances tethering of membrane vesicles and accelerates lipid transfer of ATG2 by both ATG9 and ATG13-101. Our work uncovers the molecular basis of the contact site and its assembly mechanisms imposed by the metamorphosis of ATG13-101 to regulate autophagosome biogenesis in space and time.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-19T12:41:37.734Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dyy8xfvpkf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dyy8xfvpkf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alex","last_name":"Faesen","orcid":"0000-0002-4685-1463"},{"first_name":"Björn","last_name":"Stork","orcid":"0000-0002-4167-7806"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Autophagy","Reconstitution Experiment"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5nvgrgyvww","title":"LINF_270009000","description":"Autophagy-related protein 9 | ATG9; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-10T17:10:30.971Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5nvgrgyvww","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5nvgrgyvww"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jh7h5cx4yh","title":"TNIP1 inhibits selective autophagy via bipartite interaction with LC3/GABARAP and TAX1BP1","description":"Mitophagy is a form of selective autophagy that disposes of superfluous and potentially damage-inducing organelles in a tightly controlled manner. While the machinery involved in mitophagy induction is well known, the regulation of the components is less clear. Here, we demonstrate that TNIP1 knock out accelerates mitophagy rates, and that ectopic TNIP1 negatively regulates the rate of mitophagy. These functions of TNIP1 depend on an evolutionarily conserved LIR motif as well as an AHD3 domain, which are required for binding to the LC3/GABARAP family of proteins and the autophagy receptor TAX1BP1, respectively. We further show that phosphorylation appears to regulate its association with the ULK1 complex member FIP200, allowing TNIP1 to compete with autophagy receptors, providing a molecular rationale for its inhibitory function during mitophagy. Taken together, our findings identify a novel negative regulator of mitophagy that acts at the early steps of autophagosome biogenesis. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-08T11:02:24.139Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jh7h5cx4yh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jh7h5cx4yh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"François","last_name":"Le Guerroué","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vhtn5s5dbx","title":"Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase II β (CaMKIIβ) decodes ER Ca2+ transients to trigger autophagosome formation. Qiaoxia Zheng et al.","description":"These are unprocessed and uncompressed imaging data (microscopy as well as gels and blots) of the study \"Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase II β (CaMKIIβ) decodes ER Ca2+ transients to trigger autophagosome formation\". In this study, we focused on how Ca2+ transients on the ER outer surface elicited by autophagy stimuli are sustained and decoded to trigger autophagosome formation in multicellular organisms. Here we show that CaMKIIβ integrates ER Ca2+ transients to trigger liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) of the autophagosome-initiating FIP200 complex. In response to ER Ca2+ transients, CaMKIIβ is recruited from actin filaments and forms condensates, which serve as sites for emergence of or interaction with FIP200 puncta. CaMKIIβ phosphorylates FIP200 at Thr269, Thr1127 and Ser1484 to modulate LLPS and properties of the FIP200 complex, thereby controlling its function in autophagosome formation. CaMKIIβ also controls the amplitude, duration and propagation of ER Ca2+ transients during autophagy induction. CaMKIIβ mutations identified in the neurodevelopmental disorder MRD54 affect the function of CaMKIIβ in autophagy. Our study reveals that CaMKIIβ is essential for sustaining and decoding ER Ca2+ transients to specify autophagosome formation in metazoans.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-20T12:14:55.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vhtn5s5dbx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vhtn5s5dbx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiaoxia","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32322038"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82188101"}],"categories":["Western Blot","Super-Resolution Imaging","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"259594j572","title":"ULK1 O-GlcNAcylation Is Crucial for Activating VPS34 via ATG14L during Autophagy Initiation_raw data","description":"Raw data for ULK1 O-GlcNAcylation Is Crucial for Activating VPS34 via ATG14L during Autophagy Initiation","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-11-16T06:41:43.275Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/259594j572","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/259594j572"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ki Eun","last_name":"Pyo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chang Rok ","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Minkyoung","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jong-Seo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keun Il","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sung Hee","last_name":"Baek","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Phosphatases","Autophagy","Epigenetics","Glycosylation","Kinase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4c4jcf27cp","title":"ULK1 phosphorylation of striatin activates protein phosphatase 2A  and autophagy ","description":"Raw western blot and IF data presented in the manuscript \"ULK1 phosphorylation of striatin activates protein phosphatase 2A  and autophagy\" by Zehan Hu, Devanarayanan Siva Sankar, Bich Vu, Alexandre Leytens, Christine Vionnet, Wenxian Wu, Michael Stumpe, Esther Martínez-Martínez, Björn Stork and Jörn Dengjel. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-30T06:47:24.585Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4c4jcf27cp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4c4jcf27cp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joern","last_name":"Dengjel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zehan","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fluorescence Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4gb6s7mhh7","title":"AMPK, a key regulator of metabolism and autophagy, is activated by lysosomal damage via a novel galectin-directed ubiquitin signal transduction system","description":"Original imaging data\n(microscopy and western blots) for \"AMPK, a key regulator of metabolism and autophagy, is activated by lysosomal damage via a novel galectin-directed ubiquitin signal transduction system\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-01-28T19:45:50.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4gb6s7mhh7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4gb6s7mhh7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JINGYUE","last_name":"JIA","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bhawana","last_name":"Dikshit","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"24jp9dk4gj","title":"The ULK1 effector BAG2 regulates autophagy initiation by modulating AMBRA1 localization","description":"The file contains raw western blot data of main and supplemental figures of the manuscript \"The ULK1 effector BAG2 regulates autophagy initiation by modulating AMBRA1 localization\". MW markers and uncropped membranes are shown. All biological replicates are presented and the ones used in the main manuscript are highlighted.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-11T19:06:51.456Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/24jp9dk4gj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/24jp9dk4gj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Devanarayanan ","last_name":"Siva Sankar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephanie ","last_name":"Kaeser-Pebernard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joern","last_name":"Dengjel","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00yjd3n13","name":"Swiss National Science Foundation","grant_id":"212187"}],"categories":["Biochemistry","Autophagy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":124093,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":144,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":124093,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":123523,"restricted":63,"embargoed":507},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2166},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":655},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":445},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":399},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":385},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":382},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":323},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":252},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":244}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":121634},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1752},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":180},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":127},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121d